I know exactly why this happened. This is a notorious Spring Boot "gotcha."

When we deleted the Oracle configuration but kept the `HiveDbConfig` (which creates a `DataSource`), Spring Boot's internal safety rules kicked in. **If Spring Boot detects even one manually created `DataSource` (like our Hive one), it completely disables all of its automatic database configurations.** Because of this, it completely ignored your Oracle properties and never created the `oracleJdbcTemplate`.

When an application has multiple databases, you cannot mix "automatic" and "manual." You must manually define both, but you must do it using Spring's native `DataSourceProperties` so it correctly reads the URLs and the Hikari pool settings.

Delete `JdbcTemplateConfig.java`. We are going to put the `oracleJdbcTemplate` exactly where it belongs, inside a fully bulletproof Oracle config file.

Here is the final, complete code for your two configuration files.

### 1. The Bulletproof Oracle Config

Create (or replace) this file. This reads your `spring.datasource` properties, creates the Hikari pool, and exposes the exact `oracleJdbcTemplate` your services are looking for.

**File:** `src/main/java/com/fincore/VoucherService/config/OracleDbConfig.java`

```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.config;

import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

@Configuration
public class OracleDbConfig {

    // 1. Let Spring safely parse the standard URL and credentials
    @Primary
    @Bean
    @ConfigurationProperties("spring.datasource")
    public DataSourceProperties oracleDataSourceProperties() {
        return new DataSourceProperties();
    }

    // 2. Feed those parsed properties into a strict Hikari Pool
    @Primary
    @Bean(name = "oracleDataSource")
    @ConfigurationProperties("spring.datasource.hikari")
    public HikariDataSource oracleDataSource(DataSourceProperties properties) {
        return properties.initializeDataSourceBuilder().type(HikariDataSource.class).build();
    }

    // 3. Create the exact JdbcTemplate your services are asking for
    @Primary
    @Bean(name = "oracleJdbcTemplate")
    public JdbcTemplate oracleJdbcTemplate(DataSource oracleDataSource) {
        return new JdbcTemplate(oracleDataSource);
    }
}

```

### 2. The Bulletproof Hive Config

Replace the contents of your `HiveDbConfig.java` with this. It does the exact same thing but for your secondary Data Lake.

**File:** `src/main/java/com/fincore/VoucherService/config/HiveDbConfig.java`

```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.config;

import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

@Configuration
public class HiveDbConfig {

    @Bean
    @ConfigurationProperties("spring.hive.datasource")
    public DataSourceProperties hiveDataSourceProperties() {
        return new DataSourceProperties();
    }

    @Bean(name = "hiveDataSource")
    @ConfigurationProperties("spring.hive.datasource.hikari")
    public HikariDataSource hiveDataSource(DataSourceProperties properties) {
        return properties.initializeDataSourceBuilder().type(HikariDataSource.class).build();
    }
    
    // Optional: Expose a Hive JdbcTemplate just in case your HdfsSyncService needs it
    @Bean(name = "hiveJdbcTemplate")
    public JdbcTemplate hiveJdbcTemplate(DataSource hiveDataSource) {
        return new JdbcTemplate(hiveDataSource);
    }
}

```

### 3. Verify Properties

Because this Java code perfectly utilizes Spring's native parser, your `application.properties` must use the standard `.url` format (not `.jdbc-url`).

Make sure your connection strings look exactly like this:

```properties
